            "summary": "Fall of the Khaljis, assassination of Khusrau Khan, accession of Ghazi Malik as Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq, construction of Tughlaqabad Fort, pioneering canal irrigation, conflict with Nizamuddin Auliya, and death at Afghanpur.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Downfall of the Khaljis and Rise of Ghazi Malik</h4>\n        <p>Following Alauddin's death in 1316 AD, Malik Kafur's 35-day regency ended in his assassination. Alauddin's son, <strong>Qutbuddin Mubarak Shah Khalji (1316–1320 AD)</strong>, ascended the throne. Mubarak Shah was eccentric and dissolute, notoriously appearing naked in court before his nobles and declaring himself the supreme spiritual Caliph (<em>Al-Wasiq Billah</em>). In 1320 AD, he was murdered by his favorite noble, a Gujarati convert named <strong>Khusrau Khan (Nasiruddin Khusrau Shah)</strong>, who massacred the Khalji princes.</p>\n        <p>Outraged by the usurpation, <strong>Ghazi Malik</strong>, the veteran warden of the northwestern marches and governor of Dipalpur, rallied the Muslim nobles with the battle-cry <em>'Islam in Danger'</em>. Ghazi Malik marched on Delhi, defeated and decapitated Khusrau Khan at the Battle of Hauz-i-Alai, and ascended the throne on <strong>September 8, 1320 AD</strong> as <strong>Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq Shah</strong>, founding the <strong>Tughlaq Dynasty</strong> (1320–1414 AD).</p>\n\n        <h4>Administrative Restoration and Pioneer of Canal Irrigation</h4>\n        <p>Ghiyasuddin was an experienced, pragmatic administrator who restored stability after four years of chaos:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Moderation in Taxation (Rasm-i-Mianarau):</strong> Replaced Alauddin's oppressive 50% measurement tax with a humane, moderate agrarian assessment based on sharing of actual harvest (<em>Hukm-i-Hasil</em>). He instructed revenue collectors never to increase tax by more than 1/10th or 1/11th in a single year, and exempted cultivators from taxes in times of drought.</li>\n            <li><strong>Pioneer of Artificial Canal Irrigation:</strong> Ghiyasuddin was the <strong>first Sultan in Indian history to construct state-sponsored irrigation canals</strong>, excavating channels from the Yamuna to encourage agricultural reclamation of wasteland.</li>\n            <li><strong>Restoration of Landed Privileges:</strong> Restored the hereditary perquisites and commissions of the village headmen (<em>Khots, Muqaddams</em>), provided they protected peasant cultivators.</li>\n            <li><strong>Postal System (Dak Chauki):</strong> Modernized the royal horse and foot courier post, establishing relay posts every two kos (horse post: <em>Uluq</em>; foot post: <em>Dawa</em>), enabling news from Bengal or the Deccan to reach Delhi within four days.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Tughlaqabad Fort: The Third City of Delhi</h4>\n        <p>Between 1321 and 1323 AD, Ghiyasuddin constructed <strong>Tughlaqabad</strong>, celebrated as the <strong>Third City of Delhi</strong>. Perched on a rugged rocky scarp, this massive fortress featured colossal stone walls sloping inward at a steep battering angle, pierced with narrow arrow-slits, heavy circular bastions, and underground storage vaults designed to withstand long Mongol sieges.</p>\n\n        <h4>Conflict with Nizamuddin Auliya and Death at Afghanpur (1325 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Ghiyasuddin entered into a bitter feud with the celebrated Chishti Sufi saint, <strong>Hazrat Nizamuddin Auliya</strong>:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>During his brief usurpation, Khusrau Khan had distributed five lakh tankas from the royal treasury to the city Sufis. Nizamuddin Auliya accepted the money and immediately distributed it to the poor. Upon ascending the throne, Ghiyasuddin demanded the recovery of the state funds, but the saint replied that the money had already been given to God's destitute.</li>\n            <li>While returning from a victorious military expedition in Bengal (1324–1325 AD), Ghiyasuddin sent an ultimatum from Bihar ordering Nizamuddin Auliya to leave Delhi before the Sultan's return. The saint uttered his famous prophetic reply: <em><strong>\"Hunuz Dilli Dur Ast\"</strong></em> (Delhi is yet far away).</li>\n            <li>In February 1325 AD, six miles outside Delhi at <strong>Afghanpur</strong>, Ghiyasuddin was received by his eldest son, Prince <strong>Jauna Khan (later Muhammad bin Tughlaq)</strong>. Jauna Khan had erected a hasty wooden pavilion (<em>Kushk</em>) to celebrate his father's triumph. As the royal banquet concluded and war elephants were paraded outside, the wooden roof mysteriously collapsed upon the Sultan and his younger son Prince Mahmud, crushing them to death. Contemporary chroniclers (including Ibn Battuta) openly allege that Jauna Khan engineered the collapse by having the foundation beams knocked out, ascending the throne three days later.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
