            "title": "Religious Policies & Jizya: Orthodoxy, Brahmins & Temples",
            "summary": "Firoz Shah's theological orthodoxy, dominance of conservative Sunni Ulema, first imposition of Jizya as a separate tax on Brahmins, prohibition of Muslim women visiting Sufi shrines, and destruction of the Jagannath and Jwalamukhi temples.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Orthodox Reaction: Theocracy under Firoz Shah</h4>\n        <p>If Muhammad bin Tughlaq represented the zenith of rationalism, secular statecraft, and religious tolerance, his cousin <strong>Firoz Shah Tughlaq represented the conservative theological pendulum swing</strong> toward strict Islamic orthodoxy. Born to a Hindu mother (Bibi Naila), Firoz felt a constant psychological imperative to prove his orthodox Sunni credentials to the conservative clerical establishment (<em>Ulema</em> and <em>Mashaikh</em>), elevating them to supreme ideological arbiters of the state.</p>\n\n        <h4>The Landmark Imposition of Jizya on Brahmins</h4>\n        <p>Prior to Firoz Shah, ever since the Arab conquest of Sindh in 712 AD, Brahmins had enjoyed a traditional exemption from the payment of the poll tax (<strong>Jizya</strong>), with the tax being treated as an indistinguishable surcharge bundled into the agricultural land revenue (<em>Kharaj</em>):</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Firoz consulted the ulema, who declared that Islamic law made no distinction between priestly and non-priestly infidels. Firoz decreed that <strong>Brahmins must pay Jizya as an independent, separate poll tax</strong>.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Brahmin Hunger Strike:</strong> Outraged by this loss of centuries-old religious privilege, thousands of Brahmins gathered outside Kotla Firoz Shah, threatening to starve themselves to death or immolate themselves in protest. Firoz remained completely unmoved, declaring that they were free to starve, but the tax would not be waived.</li>\n            <li>Eventually, the wealthy Hindu citizens of Delhi interceded, agreeing to pay the Jizya on behalf of the Brahmins to save their lives. The tax was fixed in three economic brackets: <strong>40 tankas, 20 tankas, and 10 tankas</strong> per annum.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Abolition of Un-Islamic Taxes & Enforcing Orthodoxy</h4>\n        <p>Guided by the Shariat, Firoz abolished over <strong>twenty-three arbitrary cesses and tolls (Abwabs)</strong> that had been collected by earlier Sultans, retaining only the four taxes sanctioned by the Quran:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>1. Kharaj:</strong> Land revenue tax (fixed at 1/10th of produce).</li>\n            <li><strong>2. Zakat:</strong> Mandatory religious charity tax levied on affluent Muslims (2.5% of savings).</li>\n            <li><strong>3. Jizya:</strong> Poll tax levied on non-Muslim subjects (Zimmis).</li>\n            <li><strong>4. Khams:</strong> One-fifth (20%) of the spoils of war surrendered to the state treasury, with the remaining four-fifths (80%) distributed to soldiers (reversing Alauddin's practice of retaining 80% for the state).</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Repression of Heretical Sects and Women's Restrictions</h4>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Persecution of Shia and Sufi Heterodoxies:</strong> Firoz suppressed the Shia Ismaili sects and executed radical Sufi mystics (e.g., Ruknuddin of Delhi and Ahmad Bihari) who preached esoteric pantheistic doctrines.</li>\n            <li><strong>Prohibition on Women:</strong> Prohibited Muslim women from visiting the tombs of Sufi saints (<em>Dargahs</em>) and public shrines, claiming it promoted moral laxity.</li>\n            <li><strong>Burning of a Brahmin at the Stake:</strong> Barani records that when a Brahmin in Delhi was accused of publicly worshipping a wooden idol and allegedly converting a Muslim woman, Firoz summoned him before the ulema; when the Brahmin refused to embrace Islam, he was burned alive at the stake outside the palace.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Destruction of Sacred Temples (Puri & Nagarkot)</h4>\n        <p>To demonstrate martial and religious zeal, Firoz conducted punitive military campaigns against prominent Hindu pilgrimage centers:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Jajpur and Puri (Odisha, 1360 AD):</strong> During his expedition to Jajnagar (Odisha), Firoz desecrated the celebrated <strong>Jagannath Temple at Puri</strong>, throwing the sacred wooden idol of Lord Jagannath into the Bay of Bengal or having it trodden underfoot in Delhi.</li>\n            <li><strong>Nagarkot / Jwalamukhi (Kangra, 1365 AD):</strong> Besieged Raja Rup Chand of Nagarkot for six months. Sacked the historic <strong>Jwalamukhi Temple</strong>. However, exhibiting his intellectual curiosity, Firoz discovered a royal library of <strong>1,300 Sanskrit manuscripts</strong> inside the temple. He ordered distinguished court scholar <strong>Aazzuddin Khalid Khani</strong> to translate selected Sanskrit works on astronomy, music, and philosophy into Persian verse under the title <strong>Dalail-i-Firoz Shahi</strong>.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
