            "title": "The Sayyid Dynasty (1414–1451 AD): Khizr Khan & Mubarak Shah",
            "summary": "Fall of the Tughlaqs (Daulat Khan Lodi), rise of the Sayyid dynasty claiming descent from Prophet Muhammad, Khizr Khan's vassal title Rayat-i-Ala, Mubarak Shah, and Yahya bin Ahmad Sirhindi's Tarikh-i-Mubarak Shahi.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>Establishment of the Sayyid Dynasty (1414 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Following the departure of Timur, the pathetic rump of the Tughlaq Sultanate dragged on until 1412 AD, when Sultan Nasiruddin Mahmud died without heirs. An Afghan noble, <strong>Daulat Khan Lodi</strong>, held authority in Delhi for two years. In March 1414 AD, <strong>Khizr Khan</strong>, who had been appointed governor of Multan and Lahore by Timur, marched on Delhi, besieged and ousted Daulat Khan, and ascended the throne on <strong>May 28, 1414 AD</strong>, founding the <strong>Sayyid Dynasty (1414–1451 AD)</strong>.</p>\n\n        <h4>The Enigma of Khizr Khan's Vassal Status (1414–1421 AD)</h4>\n        <p>The Sayyids claimed prestigious descent from <strong>Prophet Muhammad</strong> through his daughter Fatima and son-in-law Ali (hence the title <em>Sayyid</em>). However, Khizr Khan ruled with remarkable constitutional modesty:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Refusal of the Title Sultan:</strong> He never assumed royal majesty or the title of <em>Sultan</em>, ruling instead under the modest vassal title of <strong>Rayat-i-Ala</strong> ('Sublime Banners').</li>\n            <li><strong>Timurid Vassalage:</strong> He ruled ostensibly as the loyal viceroy of the Timurid empire. He did not strike coins in his own name, continuing to circulate debased Tughlaq coins or striking coins in the name of Timur's son and successor at Herat, <strong>Shah Rukh</strong>. The Friday sermon (<em>Khutba</em>) was read in the name of Timur and Shah Rukh, with Khizr Khan's name appended only as a subordinate viceroy.</li>\n            <li><strong>Annual Tribute to Herat:</strong> Khizr Khan regularly remitted annual tribute, gifts, and horses to Shah Rukh's court at Herat.</li>\n            <li>His seven-year reign was consumed entirely by annual military expeditions through Katehar, Mewat, Gwalior, Bayana, and the Doab merely to compel recalcitrant Hindu rajas and Afghan fief-holders to pay tribute.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Mubarak Shah (1421–1434 AD) and Independence</h4>\n        <p>Khizr Khan's son and successor, <strong>Muizzuddin Mubarak Shah</strong>, was the most capable ruler of the dynasty:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Unlike his father, Mubarak Shah cast off nominal Timurid vassalage. He formally declared full imperial independence, assumed the sovereign title of <strong>Sultan</strong>, struck coins in his own name, and read the Khutba exclusively in his own name, styling himself <em>Naib-i-Amir-ul-Muminin</em>.</li>\n            <li>He successfully defended Delhi against repeated invasions by <strong>Jasrat Khokhar</strong> of the Punjab and the Sharqi Sultan of Jaunpur (Ibrahim Shah Sharqi).</li>\n            <li>Founded a new royal city on the banks of the Yamuna named <strong>Mubarakabad</strong> in 1434 AD.</li>\n            <li>While inspecting the construction of Mubarakabad in February 1434 AD, he was treacherously assassinated by conspirators led by his own disgruntled Wazir, <strong>Sarwar-ul-Mulk</strong>.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Tarikh-i-Mubarak Shahi: The Primary Chronicle</h4>\n        <p>The reign of Mubarak Shah is immortalized in contemporary historiography through the celebrated historical text <strong>Tarikh-i-Mubarak Shahi</strong>, authored by <strong>Yahya bin Ahmad Sirhindi</strong>:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Dedicated to Sultan Mubarak Shah, this chronicle is the <strong>sole surviving contemporary historical source</strong> for the entire Sayyid dynasty (1414–1451 AD).</li>\n            <li>Later Mughal historians, including Nizamuddin Ahmad (<em>Tabaqat-i-Akbari</em>), Badauni (<em>Muntakhab-ut-Tawarikh</em>), and Ferishta, based their accounts of the 15th-century Sultanate almost entirely on Sirhindi's work.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Collapse of the Sayyids (1451 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Under Mubarak's weak successors, <strong>Muhammad Shah (1434–1445 AD)</strong> and <strong>Alauddin Alam Shah (1445–1451 AD)</strong>, the Sultanate shrank back to a radius of merely twenty miles around Delhi. In 1448 AD, Alam Shah permanently retired from the chaotic politics of Delhi to the peaceful gardens of <strong>Badaun</strong>, voluntarily abdicating the throne in <strong>1451 AD</strong> in favor of the powerful Afghan governor of Punjab, <strong>Bahlul Lodi</strong>, bringing the thirty-seven-year Sayyid rule to an end without bloodshed.</p>\n        ",
